  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 PM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nA cold front has pushed through the forecast area this afternoon. \nHowever, the elevated 850mb boundary remains parked over parts of \nmainly northwest Iowa. Starting to see some echoes on radar begin to \ndevelop along this portion of the elevated boundary as a mid level \nwave is advancing across the boundary within the right entrance \nregion of an upper level jet. Omega (upward motion) has not \nstrengthened within the dendritic growth zone (DGZ) yet, but should \nsee some improvement this evening. This combination of forcing will \nresult in rain developing and persisting across this area this \nevening before exiting around midnight. This will leave rainfall \namounts between a tenth to a quarter of an inch along and east of \nhighway-60.\n\nSunday will be the first day with the cooler, post frontal airmass \nin place. Highs will only warm to the 30s to low 40s, with breezy \nnorthwest winds in place. These winds will result in wind chills \ndown to near to below freezing for the entire day despite a return \nof sunny skies. Lows will fall to the teens and 20s overnight. Cold \nhigh pressure will slide through the forecast area on Monday, \nkeeping highs a little bit colder in the 30s. Winds will be much \nlighter though not calm. They will be just elevated enough to again \nresult in wind chills below freezing. Lows again will fall to the \nteens and 20s overnight.\n\nWinds will turn to out of the southeast ahead of an incoming \nshortwave trough on Tuesday. Highs will remain in the 30s with wind \nchills down to the 20s for the day. The next chance for \nprecipitation will come Tuesday night into Wednesday morning as the \nwave begins to push into the forecast area. Medium range guidance \nshows that the DGZ will be saturated but with drier conditions \nresiding in the low levels. This is supported by the ensembles as \nthey show a 20-50% chance for a hundredth of an inch of \nprecipitation. Thermal profiles will be cold enough to support all \nsnow. As of now, looks like the dry air will be the main limiting \nfactor preventing any appreciate snowfall from occurring.\n\nVariance in the upper level pattern increases for the end of next \nweek. With a strong southern jet in place, dry conditions look to \npersist for the Northern Plains. Highs look to remain near \nseasonable for this period of time as well.",
